1.20.0 fetch adapter sets cache: 'default' on every Request → TypeError: Unsupported cache mode on Cloudflare Workers

### Describe the bug

Since 1.20.0 the `fetch` adapter fills every `Request` it constructs with a fixed set of browser defaults (`DEFAULT_REQUEST_OPTIONS` in `lib/adapters/fetch.js`, introduced by #11141), including `cache: 'default'`.

Cloudflare Workers (workerd) only accept `no-store` / `no-cache` for the `cache` option and throw at `Request` construction for anything else. As a result **every** request made through the fetch adapter on Workers now fails before a single byte is sent:

```
TypeError: Unsupported cache mode: default
```

1.19.0 works, because it only passed the options it was given.

Related workerd limitation: https://github.com/cloudflare/workerd/issues/698, https://github.com/cloudflare/workerd/issues/2426.

Note: frameworks that shim `globalThis.Request` (e.g. `@opennextjs/cloudflare` strips `cache` inside its fetch handler) mask the bug there, which makes it surface only in queue consumers / cron handlers of the same Worker. That is how we hit it in production.

### To Reproduce

`worker.mjs`:

```js
import axios from 'axios'

export default {
  async fetch() {
    try {
      const res = await axios.get('https://cloudflare.com/cdn-cgi/trace', { adapter: 'fetch' })
      return new Response(`ok ${res.status}`)
    } catch (e) {
      return new Response(String(e))
    }
  },
}
```

`wrangler.jsonc`:

```jsonc
{ "name": "axios-cache-mode", "main": "worker.mjs", "compatibility_date": "2026-05-26" }
```

```
wrangler dev --local
curl http://127.0.0.1:8787/
# → TypeError: Unsupported cache mode: default
```

Same snippet with `axios@1.19.0` → `ok 200`.

### Code snippet

The relevant part of `lib/adapters/fetch.js` (1.20.0):

```js
const DEFAULT_REQUEST_OPTIONS = {
  cache: 'default',
  redirect: 'follow',
  referrer: 'about:client',
  // ...
};

if (isRequestSupported) {
  utils.forEach(DEFAULT_REQUEST_OPTIONS, (value, key) => {
    if (resolvedOptions[key] === undefined) {
      resolvedOptions[key] = value;
    }
  });
  // ...
}

request = isRequestSupported && new Request(url, resolvedOptions);
```

### Expected behavior

Options the caller did not set should not be sent to the runtime's `Request` constructor, or at least `cache` should be left undefined (the spec default is already `'default'`, so setting it explicitly buys nothing and breaks runtimes that reject it).

### Workaround

Pass an explicit mode the runtime accepts:

```js
axios.create({ adapter: 'fetch', fetchOptions: { cache: 'no-store' } })
```

### Axios Version

1.20.0 (regression from 1.19.0)

### Adapter Version

fetch

### Browser / Runtime

Cloudflare Workers (workerd), reproduced with wrangler 4.127.0, `compatibility_date` 2026-05-26 (with and without `nodejs_compat`)

### Node.js Version

26.7.0 (host running wrangler)

### OS

macOS 15 (host); workerd runtime

### Additional Library Versions

_No response_

### Additional context/Screenshots

The same `Request` init applied one key at a time in workerd: only `cache: 'default'` throws; `mode`, `referrer`, `referrerPolicy`, `priority`, `window`, `integrity`, `keepalive`, `redirect` are accepted.

XHR adapter resolves navigation-canceled requests as success on Firefox 152+ (status 0, empty body)

## Describe the issue

axios contradicts itself about what `status === 0` means, and Firefox 152 made the disagreement reachable.

The XHR adapter has the rule right, and scopes the `file:` exception properly:

```js
// lib/adapters/xhr.js, the onreadystatechange fallback
// The request errored out and we didn't get a response, this will be
// handled by onerror instead
// With one exception: request that using file: protocol, most browsers
// will return status as 0 even though it's a successful request
if (request.status === 0 && !(request.responseURL && request.responseURL.startsWith('file:'))) {
  return;
}
```

`settle()` does not scope it at all. It resolves any status-0 response as a success, on any scheme:

```js
// lib/core/settle.js
if (!response.status || !validateStatus || validateStatus(response.status)) {
  resolve(response);
}
```

The catch is where that adapter guard lives. It sits in the `onreadystatechange` fallback, which only runs when `'onloadend' in request` is false, and that is never the case in a current browser. Every evergreen browser takes the `onloadend` path instead, which has no equivalent check, so the unscoped version in `settle()` is the one that actually runs.

For about six years this did not matter, because browsers always fired `error` or `abort` before `loadend` on a failed or canceled XHR. `onabort`/`onerror` would reject and set `request = null`, and `onloadend` would bail on `if (!request) return` before `settle()` ever ran.

Firefox 152 changed that. [Bugzilla 1505389](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1505389) made navigation-canceled XHRs fire only `readystatechange` and `loadend`, with no `abort` and no `error`. The patch says it outright:

> Don't make navigation block XHR and call onerror/abort
>
> Fires readystatechange and loadend intead of abort

It is intentional and it has been upstreamed. Mozilla landed a conformance test in web-platform-tests ([wpt#59603](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/pull/59603), `xhr/abort-during-navigation.window.js`) asserting that `error`, `abort` and `load` must not fire. Chrome already fires nothing and passes it. Safari still fires `abort` and would fail it. So this looks like where the platform is heading rather than a Firefox quirk that will get reverted.

Note also that `!response.status` is evaluated before `validateStatus`, so this cannot be worked around with config.

## Example code

Any request that is in flight when the user navigates away, on Firefox 152 or later:

```js
// Page navigates (link click, location assignment, reload) while this is pending.
const { data } = await axios.get('/api/things');
// Firefox <= 151: rejects with ECONNABORTED, because the adapter saw an abort event.
// Firefox >= 152: RESOLVES, with status 0 and data === ''.
// Anything parsing or validating `data` then fails on an empty string.
```

Reproducible without a browser, by driving the real xhr adapter with a stub `XMLHttpRequest`. The only difference between the two runs is whether `error` fires:

```js
let SEQUENCE = [];
globalThis.XMLHttpRequest = class {
  constructor() {
    this.readyState = 0; this.status = 0; this.statusText = '';
    this.responseText = ''; this.response = ''; this.timeout = 0;
    this.onloadend = this.onabort = this.onerror = this.ontimeout = this.onreadystatechange = null;
  }
  open() {} setRequestHeader() {} abort() {} getAllResponseHeaders() { return ''; }
  send() {
    setImmediate(() => {
      this.readyState = 4;
      for (const ev of SEQUENCE) this[`on${ev}`]?.call(this, { type: ev });
    });
  }
};
const { default: axios } = await import('axios');

for (const [label, seq] of [
  ['error precedes loadend (every browser before FF152)', ['readystatechange', 'error', 'loadend']],
  ['loadend only        (Firefox >= 152 nav-cancel)     ', ['readystatechange', 'loadend']],
]) {
  SEQUENCE = seq;
  cons
